<p>Define a slice knot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the number of crossings in a particular diagram need not equal
the crossing number of the knot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Which Reidemeister move changes the crossing number of a diagram by one?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Which Reidemeister move introduces or removes two crossings?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Which Reidemeister move preserves the number of crossings?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose mutual crossings of two oriented link components have signs</p>

<p>
<md>+1,+1,-1,+1,-1,+1.</md>
</p>

<p>Compute their linking number.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why nonzero linking number proves that a two-component link is
nontrivial.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why zero linking number does not prove that a link is trivial.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>What is the knot group of the unknot?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a knot with nonabelian knot group cannot be the unknot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Fox <m>n</m>-coloring relation.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why three-colorability distinguishes the trefoil from the unknot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\Delta_K(t)=t^2-3t+1,</md>
</p>

<p>calculate <m>\det(K)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\Delta_K(t)=1,</md>
</p>

<p>can you conclude that <m>K</m> is the unknot? Explain.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose a Seifert algorithm produces</p>

<p>
<md>c=9</md>
</p>

<p>crossings and</p>

<p>
<md>s=6</md>
</p>

<p>Seifert circles. Determine the genus of the resulting surface.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute</p>

<p>
<md>g(T(2,7)).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute</p>

<p>
<md>g(T(4,5)).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute</p>

<p>
<md>c(T(2,9)).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether <m>T(4,6)</m> is a knot or a link.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine the number of components of <m>T(4,6)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use Reidemeister moves to simplify several diagrams of the unknot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct a nontrivial Fox <m>3</m>-coloring of a trefoil diagram.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive a Wirtinger presentation for the trefoil knot group.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the trefoil group</p>

<p>
<md>\langle a,b\mid aba=bab\rangle</md>
</p>

<p>is nonabelian.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the abelianization of the trefoil knot group is <m>\Z</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the abelianization of every knot group is <m>\Z</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the Seifert algorithm on an oriented trefoil diagram and determine the
genus of the resulting surface.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>g(K_1\#K_2)
=
g(K_1)+g(K_2).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the Alexander polynomial is multiplicative under connected sum,
up to units.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify the braid relation</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1
=
\sigma_2\sigma_1\sigma_2</md>
</p>

<p>geometrically using braid diagrams.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write the trefoil as the closure of a braid.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the closure of</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_1^3\in B_2</md>
</p>

<p>is a trefoil knot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why <m>T(p,q)</m> has <m>\gcd(p,q)</m> components.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute the crossing number and genus of</p>

<p>
<md>T(5,7).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why writhe fails to be invariant under all Reidemeister moves.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Describe how the Kauffman bracket can be normalized using writhe.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the knot exterior has torus boundary.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Describe the meridian and preferred longitude of a knot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain geometrically how Dehn surgery modifies a knot exterior.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a hyperbolic knot has a well-defined hyperbolic volume.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compare torus, satellite, and hyperbolic knots.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study a proof of the Reidemeister Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research the prime decomposition theorem for knots.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study the Wirtinger presentation using the Seifert--van Kampen Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct the Alexander polynomial from a presentation of the Alexander
module.</p>
